syntax: parse unknown @ expansion operators as bash does

Bash checks the operator of ${name@op} only when it expands the
parameter, not when it parses the script: bash -n accepts any operator,
an unset parameter expands to nothing, and only a set one fails with
"bad substitution". The parser rejected every operator outside the known
set, so a typo or a template placeholder such as

	if [ -z "${FLAGS_SET_@suffix@:-}" ]; then

stopped the parse, and tools that keep parsing after errors lost the
rest of the file. An unknown operator now parses into an Expansion like
any other, leaving its validation to tools that know which shell will
run the script; a non-literal operator is still an error.

Reproducer, before this change: `echo ${a@bar}` with LangBash failed
with "invalid @ expansion operator `bar`".

